1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:09,327
The brush clears a set of balance scales, the kind a trader used for gold. Under the Guptas, trade in gold and cloth was brisk.

2
00:00:09,327 --> 00:00:15,357
But the golden age was not golden for everyone. Did everyone share in the golden age?

3
00:00:31,517 --> 00:00:36,454
How do we know? Inscriptions record land grants and local events.

4
00:00:36,454 --> 00:00:41,729
The Smritis, law books such as Narada's, set out the rules of society.

5
00:00:41,729 --> 00:00:47,384
Gupta coins show how rich the state was, and how the gold content changed.

6
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:05,928
The Guptas ruled through a mix of direct and indirect control.

7
00:01:05,928 --> 00:01:11,100
Many local rulers, called samantas, paid tribute and kept their lands.

8
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:19,186
The empire was divided into provinces called bhuktis, and districts called vishayas. Land grants became common.

9
00:01:19,186 --> 00:01:25,296
Kings gave villages, tax-free, to Brahmins and temples, in grants called agraharas.

10
00:01:25,296 --> 00:01:31,952
This gave grantees rights over the land and its people. Trade in the Gupta age was strong at first.

11
00:01:31,952 --> 00:01:35,750
Guilds, called shrenis, organised crafts.

12
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:43,293
But after about the fifth century, the gold content of coins fell, and some scholars see this as a sign of change in trade.

13
00:01:43,293 --> 00:01:51,915
The Smritis tightened the rules of society. Untouchables, called chandalas, lived outside the towns, as Fa-Hien noted.

14
00:01:51,915 --> 00:02:00,591
The earliest clear record of sati comes from an inscription at Eran in 510 CE. Women's property rights were limited.

15
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:24,093
Scholars disagree. Was the Gupta age a golden age? One view says yes, because of peace, art, science and trade.

16
00:02:24,093 --> 00:02:32,331
Another view says the label hides inequality, falling trade late in the age, and the harder position of women and low castes.

17
00:02:32,331 --> 00:02:37,154
Historians now mostly say it was an age of achievement and of change.

18
00:02:51,817 --> 00:03:03,151
Carve this tip into memory. Samantas, agraharas, shrenis. Smritis, Eran 510. Five terms from Gupta society.

19
00:03:03,151 --> 00:03:07,277
Match each to one change, and your answer is ready.

20
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:38,470
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. What were tax-free village grants to Brahmins called? The answer: agraharas. Number two.

21
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:56,219
What were the merchant guilds called? The answer: shrenis. Number three. Where is the earliest record of sati, dated 510 CE?

22
00:03:56,219 --> 00:04:06,166
The answer: Eran. Now one line for Mains. Examine the social and economic changes of the Gupta period.

23
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:34,289
The scales go into their case. They weighed gold, and a changing economy. Three facts to take away. Scales used for weighing gold.

24
00:04:34,289 --> 00:04:43,167
Shrenis organised trade and craft. Land grants shaped society. Next gallery: Science and Letters.

